00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:13.947
Valley bank and Signature Bank

00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:14.514
last month.

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:15.482
We went down to Atlanta

00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:16.683
to sit down with him,

00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:17.550
where he was taking part

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.352
in a community center branch opening

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:21.621
in the historic district of Summerhill,

00:00:23.556 --> 00:00:24.924
one mortgage

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:27.894
and one business, one account.

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:28.762
It gets sold.

00:00:28.762 --> 00:00:30.697
The saver helps you get a job.

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:31.998
So one skills.

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:33.266
And we do a lot of work with community

00:00:33.266 --> 00:00:35.502
around community colleges and skills and

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:36.870
and affordable housing.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.506
And you can lift up society

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.375
This is

00:00:42.509 --> 00:00:44.644
the community center branch here,

00:00:44.644 --> 00:00:46.079
but you have a lot of them.

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:47.881
You opened the first one in Harlem

00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:49.582
in New York City in 2019

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:51.684
you've got 16 cities now. Atlanta.

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:52.952
Why do you do this?

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:54.454
Yeah, this is a great idea.

00:00:54.454 --> 00:00:56.122
Someone had was a mine

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:57.090
for the community branch

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.159
so it's bigger as a community space

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:01.261
it's built by local contractors

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:01.861
think of minority

00:01:01.861 --> 00:01:03.763
contractors, local food,

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:05.965
local art artists

00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:08.435
you know people put the art up when we

00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:09.903
we hire locally

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:11.438
we have what we call community managers.

00:01:11.438 --> 00:01:13.173
You have a branch manager traditional

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:14.040
for the community manager

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.775
get to know all the local businesses

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:17.844
not for profit religious institutions.

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:19.112
We invite people in

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:21.247
to learn about mortgages and saving money

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:23.149
and starting a business

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:24.751
and it's been unbelievable.

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:26.986
I've been like 14 of the 16 or so.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.055
We've always taken the community manager.

00:01:29.055 --> 00:01:31.257
We have 120 that's works so well.

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:31.658
We're putting them

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:32.759
in a lot of different communities.

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:33.960
So that's just

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:34.994
part of what we try to do that

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:36.029
when we enter a town,

00:01:36.029 --> 00:01:37.330
we have 30% of our branches.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.666
So in low income neighborhoods

00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:41.401
and we just try to lift our neighborhoods

00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:42.535
and bring in all the things

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:44.070
that JP Morgan Chase can do to help.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.238
One of the things you say though,

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:46.372
because this is part of

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:47.640
the $30 billion commitment

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:48.908
to black and brown communities

00:01:48.908 --> 00:01:49.843
across the country,

00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:51.377
but you hear you explicitly

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:53.446
say this isn't charity.

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:55.048
Yeah, there's a strong business case.

00:01:55.048 --> 00:01:56.850
Yeah, that's totally true.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:57.684
And there's two things.

00:01:57.684 --> 00:01:59.819
So those are huge numbers that we say.

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:01.621
12 billion of affordable housing.

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:02.822
8 billion in mortgages.

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:03.423
But at the end of the day

00:02:03.423 --> 00:02:05.525
it takes place in a local a local place.

00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:07.327
The mortgage loan officer know

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:08.394
reaching out to people,

00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:08.862
helping them

00:02:08.862 --> 00:02:09.629
through the programs,

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:11.397
getting them a homeowner's grant

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:12.632
if they need something like that.

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:13.533
So that's how it takes place.

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:14.767
But when you do it,

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:15.401
you know, we're right

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:17.403
across from Georgia State University

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:18.104
they're businesses

00:02:18.104 --> 00:02:19.539
opening up around here, partially

00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:21.141
because every branch in here

00:02:21.141 --> 00:02:22.208
and a list up the community.

00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:23.810
But it's also good business.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.011
And you want good business

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:28.014
as these businesses come here they are

00:02:28.014 --> 00:02:28.848
they're hiring locally,

00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:30.450
their paychecks in the neighborhood

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.684
and a list of everybody.

00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:33.453
So that's part of the job of a bank.

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:34.254
What's interesting,

00:02:34.254 --> 00:02:34.787
though, about this

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:36.322
time, exactly this moment

00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:37.157
that we're sitting down

00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:38.091
is we're in a rising

00:02:38.091 --> 00:02:39.792
interest rate environment

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:40.560
and it is harder

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:41.895
for folks to get mortgages.

00:02:41.895 --> 00:02:43.196
You've lamented

00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:44.898
that reality, especially for low,

00:02:44.898 --> 00:02:45.231
lower

00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:46.266
income individuals,

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:48.001
especially for lower income individuals

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:49.235
in this country right now.

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:49.702
Do you think

00:02:49.702 --> 00:02:51.804
given the high prices of homes now,

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:54.007
the rising interest rate environment,

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:56.009
this American dream of owning

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:56.409
a home

00:02:56.409 --> 00:02:59.078
is really no longer for a lot of folks.

00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:00.813
I think we have to bring it back.

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:01.748
You know, if you look at homes

00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:03.516
that are for most people,

00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:04.517
the way you start building

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:06.786
family net worth and family wealth,

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.354
and there are a lot of things to do.

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:08.655
And sure,

00:03:08.655 --> 00:03:09.455
you already see home

00:03:09.455 --> 00:03:11.257
prices are down in some places.

00:03:11.257 --> 00:03:13.326
Builders are already making concessions.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.094
We do a lot of special things,

00:03:15.094 --> 00:03:16.062
you know,
special purpose

00:03:16.062 --> 00:03:17.530
credit for sometimes

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.299
in low income neighborhoods.

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:19.966
I think the government

00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:20.733
can do a lot of things.

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:21.334
So, you know,

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:22.268
we talk about these things here,

00:03:22.268 --> 00:03:23.570
but the government can do a lot of things

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.371
to reduce the cost they're doing now.

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:25.805
Absolutely.

00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:26.873
What reducing

00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:27.707
production costs,

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:29.309
servicing costs, origination

00:03:29.309 --> 00:03:29.976
costs,

00:03:29.976 --> 00:03:31.377
securitization costs,

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:32.579
which would make the cost

00:03:32.579 --> 00:03:34.013
of a small mortgage,

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:35.882
but probably 50 to 75 basis points

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:37.283
cheaper than a loan.

00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:38.384
A lot of money a month.

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:39.452
That's a lot of money a month

00:03:39.452 --> 00:03:40.820
and make it available to more people.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.922
So and we try to take policies,

00:03:42.922 --> 00:03:44.090
you know, things we do here,

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:44.657
but also worry

00:03:44.657 --> 00:03:45.091
about what

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:46.359
Washington can do to make it easier

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:46.960
to accomplish.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:48.127
Listening to you,

00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:49.329
I've been digging in this one

00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:49.996
for a long time.

00:03:49.996 --> 00:03:51.331
I'm praying, I'm begging.

00:03:51.331 --> 00:03:52.932
I hope they'll listen to this.

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:53.766
It's so important.

00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:55.201
And the other important thing is

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:57.070
we're not doing it for JPMorgan.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:58.238
It won't affect the future.

00:03:58.238 --> 00:03:59.672
JPMorgan, if we make mortgages

00:03:59.672 --> 00:04:00.540
more available

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.308
to lower income folks around America,

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:03.343
it's so important

00:04:03.343 --> 00:04:04.043
for lower income

00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:05.445
America and so, you know,

00:04:05.445 --> 00:04:06.479
we won't stop pushing

00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:07.880
this one till we get it done.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:09.782
I think we should double the

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:11.284
earned income tax credit.

00:04:11.284 --> 00:04:12.318
And then there is Warren

00:04:12.318 --> 00:04:14.254
Buffett has been saying that for years.

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:15.455
So if the American public,

00:04:15.455 --> 00:04:18.591
if you if someone if a single parent is

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:20.193
with two kids

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:21.761
is making $14,000

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:23.963
a year,
the government gives you six roughly.

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:25.398
If you're don't have children

00:04:25.398 --> 00:04:26.866
getting about $600.

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:27.533
My view is

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:28.601
we need to get more income

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:29.202
into neighborhoods.

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:29.836
So if you

00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:31.504
if you kind of add to that earned income

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:32.071
tax credit,

00:04:32.071 --> 00:04:33.773
I would get rid of the child

00:04:33.773 --> 00:04:35.341
requirement requirement

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:36.476
or you get more money

00:04:36.476 --> 00:04:37.076
into the neighborhoods

00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:38.845
and that money will be well spent.

00:04:38.845 --> 00:04:40.980
It will lift up those communities.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.315
There'll be nothing like that.

00:04:42.315 --> 00:04:42.782
And the other thing

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:44.617
I remember is job to bring dignity

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:46.653
jobs, bring better social outcomes,

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:47.820
you know, jobs,

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:48.588
that first job,

00:04:48.588 --> 00:04:49.289
because sometimes people

00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:51.357
make fun of the burger flipper job.

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:52.558
Very often that burger

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:53.626
flipper own ends up

00:04:53.626 --> 00:04:55.261
owning the restaurant. Done it.

00:04:55.261 --> 00:04:55.862
Exactly.

00:04:55.862 --> 00:04:56.529
We've all done it

00:04:56.529 --> 00:04:57.530
in that first job,

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:58.598
that first rung on the ladder.

00:04:58.598 --> 00:04:59.732
Have you done it?

00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:01.567
No, but I served in the restaurant.

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:04.037
Yes. I used to be a waitress.

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:05.805
We're
just getting people to open account.

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:06.673
We've got a counter

00:05:06.673 --> 00:05:08.608
you could open here, start saving money,

00:05:08.608 --> 00:05:09.542
thinking about it.

00:05:09.542 --> 00:05:10.710
You know, a lot of us had the benefit.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:11.210
Their mom

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.279
or dad took us to a branch

00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:14.947
and we saw the first pass for that.

00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:16.082
And then you see your money

00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:20.186
go from like $84.75 to $85.17.

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:22.155
So, like, that was magic. There's a God

00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:24.023
interest

00:05:24.023 --> 00:05:24.424
and so

00:05:24.424 --> 00:05:25.058
people learn,

00:05:25.058 --> 00:05:27.060
and we want that to take place here.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.594
You know,
we don't want people to be afraid

00:05:28.594 --> 00:05:30.163
to walk into a branch here.

00:05:30.163 --> 00:05:32.332
You come as you are, bring your kids,

00:05:32.332 --> 00:05:33.366
you know,
learn what you're doing

00:05:33.366 --> 00:05:34.867
and that's why
the community marriage is so important,

00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:36.069
because a lot of people,

00:05:36.069 --> 00:05:36.936
you know,
look at a bank

00:05:36.936 --> 00:05:39.639
and they're going to walk by.

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:40.907
It's not what they're comfortable with.

00:05:40.907 --> 00:05:42.308
And we want this community

00:05:42.308 --> 00:05:44.010
very comfortable walking in here.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.378
I just read your annual letter

00:05:45.378 --> 00:05:47.013
that just came out this week.

00:05:47.013 --> 00:05:49.649
And let's dove into the banking crisis,

00:05:49.649 --> 00:05:51.050
because you write a lot about it.

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:53.920
Is the current banking crisis over I

00:05:54.087 --> 00:05:56.189
this is not 2008, okay.

00:05:56.222 --> 00:05:58.091
This is much more limited.

00:05:58.091 --> 00:05:59.325
There are only a handful of banks

00:05:59.325 --> 00:06:01.527
that have this particular problem.

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:02.929
They'll eventually be resolved

00:06:02.929 --> 00:06:04.063
one way or another.

00:06:04.063 --> 00:06:04.597
And I think

00:06:04.597 --> 00:06:05.998
people should take a deep breath

00:06:05.998 --> 00:06:07.467
in a week or two.

00:06:07.467 --> 00:06:08.167
A lot of these banks

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:09.235
have been reporting earnings.

00:06:09.235 --> 00:06:11.003
I think they're probably pretty good.

00:06:11.003 --> 00:06:11.771
The Federal Reserve

00:06:11.771 --> 00:06:12.338
made some

00:06:12.405 --> 00:06:13.473
bold dramatic moves

00:06:13.473 --> 00:06:14.307
to help it easier

00:06:14.307 --> 00:06:16.008
for some of the issues they had.

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:17.810
And I'm hoping it will resolve,

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:18.778
you know, rather shortly.

00:06:18.778 --> 00:06:20.146
You're hoping hoping.

00:06:20.146 --> 00:06:21.280
But is the crisis over?

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:22.081
You wrote in your letter

00:06:22.081 --> 00:06:23.316
there will be repercussions

00:06:23.316 --> 00:06:24.650
for years to come.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:25.551
Well, that's that's different.

00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:26.018
I think

00:06:26.018 --> 00:06:27.920
those repercussions are regulatory

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:28.521
like and,

00:06:28.521 --> 00:06:29.288
you know,
acknowledged

00:06:29.288 --> 00:06:30.556
think obviously we have a problem

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:31.824
and things need to change.

00:06:31.824 --> 00:06:33.760
But you know, begging the regulars, let's

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:35.161
just take a deep breath.

00:06:35.161 --> 00:06:36.763
There are hundreds of rules.

00:06:36.763 --> 00:06:37.964
You know, you have to be very careful.

00:06:37.964 --> 00:06:39.565
What do you want in the banking system?

00:06:39.565 --> 00:06:40.600
What do you want out?

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.001
How do you make it easier for community

00:06:42.001 --> 00:06:43.836
banks and regional banks?

00:06:43.836 --> 00:06:45.371
How do you reduce their cost down,

00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:46.773
increase their costs on it,

00:06:46.773 --> 00:06:48.241
but also make it safe?

00:06:48.241 --> 00:06:50.276
Do you expect more

00:06:50.276 --> 00:06:51.711
banks to fail this year?

00:06:51.711 --> 00:06:52.545
I don't know.

00:06:52.545 --> 00:06:53.746
But if there are, I know

00:06:53.746 --> 00:06:55.415
honestly, they'll be resolved in

00:06:55.415 --> 00:06:56.749
probably the last of them.

00:06:56.749 --> 00:06:57.250
I think

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:58.084
I think we're getting near

00:06:58.084 --> 00:06:58.418
the end

00:06:58.418 --> 00:07:01.053
of this particular crisis and fewer

00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:02.522
finances to remember.

00:07:02.522 --> 00:07:03.289
In 08,

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:04.857
it was hundreds of institutes

00:07:04.857 --> 00:07:06.759
around the world, far too much leverage.

00:07:06.759 --> 00:07:08.528
We don't have that huge problems

00:07:08.528 --> 00:07:10.396
in mortgage markets. We don't have that.

00:07:10.396 --> 00:07:11.531
This is nothing like that.

00:07:11.531 --> 00:07:12.632
And the American public

00:07:12.632 --> 00:07:14.700
shouldn't think that this will resolve.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:15.301
And then we should go

00:07:15.301 --> 00:07:17.503
look at what went wrong and fix it,

00:07:17.503 --> 00:07:18.671
you know, in the clean

00:07:18.671 --> 00:07:20.006
in the light of day.

00:07:20.006 --> 00:07:21.707
Well, so then then what was it?

00:07:21.707 --> 00:07:23.176
If this is coming to an end,

00:07:23.176 --> 00:07:24.710
is this a situation like, you know,

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.078
Warren Buffett famously said

00:07:26.078 --> 00:07:27.280
only when the tide goes out,

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:27.647
you learn

00:07:27.647 --> 00:07:29.282
who's been swimming naked

00:07:29.282 --> 00:07:31.050
